<h2>Understanding the Payment Math</h2>
<p>Even an accurate estimate can produce a check that looks wrong. Most replacement cost claims are paid in stages:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Replacement cost value (RCV):</strong> the estimated cost to repair or replace with like kind and quality.</li>
<li><strong>Less depreciation:</strong> the result is actual cash value (ACV).</li>
<li><strong>Less the deductible:</strong> on many Texas policies, a separate wind/hail deductible written as a percentage of the dwelling limit.</li>
<li><strong>Less prior payments.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Recoverable depreciation:</strong> often released after repairs are completed, within any time limit in the policy.</li>
</ol>
<p>Some Texas policies pay roofs only at actual cash value or use a roof payment schedule, which can explain a low roof payment even when the estimate is complete. Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> lets you test your own numbers, and our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> explains the difference.</p>

