<h2>Reopening a Previously Settled Claim</h2>
<p>Many property owners assume a paid claim is closed for good. In practice, insurers often reopen claims when new, relevant information comes in, and many policies set a time limit for doing so. A previously settled claim may be worth a second look if:</p>
<ul>
<li>Repairs uncovered hidden damage, such as wet decking under shingles or rotted framing behind siding.</li>
<li>Damage appeared later that traces back to the same storm or event.</li>
<li>The original estimate left out items or used quantities that turned out to be wrong.</li>
<li>Actual repair costs came in well above the estimate for the same scope.</li>
</ul>
<p>Reopening is a request, not a right to a particular result. The insurer will review whether the new items relate to the original loss and whether policy conditions were met. Two things can limit your options: deadlines written into the policy, and any release you signed at settlement. Gather your original estimate, payment letters, and new documentation, then ask the insurer in writing about reopening. Our <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a> page covers how additional damage is usually presented.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/xactimate-estimate-review-desk.webp" alt="Adjuster at two monitors reviewing an estimate and roof photos" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Comparing the insurer's estimate with photos and measurements, line by line, shows exactly where the numbers differ.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How We Approach an Underpaid Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Collect the claim file:</strong> the full estimate, photo report, payment letters, and policy.</li>
<li><strong>Re-inspect the property</strong> and document missed or misdescribed damage.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare our own Xactimate estimate</strong> in the same format as the insurer's, so differences are easy to see. See <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Submit and follow up</strong> with the documentation supporting each disputed item.</li>
<li><strong>Discuss appraisal</strong> if negotiation stalls and the only disagreement is the amount of loss. See <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Local factors matter. Post-storm demand can push Central Texas repair prices up for months. Hill Country metal roofs, stone veneer, and older homes near downtown may need specialized trades. And the City of San Marcos has enforced the 2021 International Codes since April 1, 2023, which can affect what a compliant repair includes. See our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page.</p>

<h2>Repair vs. Replace and Matching</h2>
<p>A frequent source of underpayment is the choice between repairing and replacing. An estimate may approve a handful of shingles, a few siding boards, or one room of flooring. That can be appropriate when matching material is available and the surrounding material is sound. It becomes harder to support when the product is discontinued, when older shingles crack as they are lifted, or when new material would plainly not match the color or profile next to it.</p>
<p>Policies treat matching differently, and the answer depends on the wording and the facts. Helpful evidence includes photos of the surrounding material, supplier or manufacturer information on product availability, and a contractor's written explanation of why a spot repair is or is not practical. Specific evidence gives the insurer a concrete reason to reconsider, rather than a general request for more money. A San Marcos public adjuster can help assemble it.</p>

<h2>Before You Accept or Sign Anything</h2>
<p>Read every document carefully, especially anything labeled a release or final settlement. Keep copies of all estimates and letters. If you believe the insurer acted in bad faith or you are close to a deadline to sue, consult a Texas attorney; public adjusters do not give legal advice. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
<p>If you want a licensed <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> to compare your settlement with the damage on the ground, start with a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
